  "intro": "The U.S. exported **$9.33B** of ores, concentrates, slag and ash in 2025, up 10.9% from 2024. Copper ores and concentrates (2603) were the largest product at **$3.54B (38%)**, followed by zinc concentrate (2608, $1.47B), molybdenum concentrate (2613, $1.42B, more than half of it roasted moly oxide), gold and silver ores and concentrates (2616, $849M) and lead concentrate (2607, $846M). Iron ore, mostly taconite pellets, fell by half to $527M. Mexico ($2.42B, nearly all copper concentrate) and Canada ($2.40B: copper, zinc and lead concentrate and iron ore pellets) took **52%**. China bought $369M, down 71% from $1.29B in 2024, while exports to South Korea doubled to $1.01B. Exports are up **20.3%** in 2026 so far: $6.89B from January to August.\n\nTariffs matter in one market. **China** charges U.S. ores a 0% normal rate, plus the 10% extra duty on all U.S. goods since May 14, 2025, plus a 2018–2019 counter-tariff of up to 25% unless the Chinese importer holds an exclusion. So U.S. copper and zinc concentrate pay **35%**, lead and molybdenum concentrate 20% and iron ore fines 10%. Every other large buyer charges **0%** on U.S. ores and concentrates: Mexico and Canada under USMCA, Korea under KORUS, and the EU, Japan, the UK and Taiwan on every supplier. There, access turns on the buyer's rules for metal-bearing residues and waste and, for EU buyers of iron ore pellets, the EU carbon border mechanism (CBAM). On the U.S. side, uranium and thorium ores need an NRC export licence, and slag, ash and residues that are hazardous waste need EPA's consent before they leave.",
