  "keyTakeaways": [
    "U.S. exports of ores, slag and ash were $9.33B in 2025 (+10.9%) and $6.89B in January–August 2026 (+20.3%). Copper concentrate is 38% of the total, and almost two-thirds of it goes to Mexico.",
    "China is the only large buyer that charges U.S. ores more than in January 2025: 35% on copper, zinc and nickel concentrate and iron ore pellets unless the importer holds an exclusion. The exclusions end December 31, 2026, and the suspension of 24 points of China's extra duty ends January 10, 2027.",
    "Mexico, Canada, the EU, Korea, Japan, the UK and Taiwan charge 0% on every Chapter 26 ore. No buyer except China has put a counter-tariff on U.S. ores: Canada's 2025 and 2026 lists skipped them, and the EU's suspended list named copper ore only at 0%.",
    "Most ores need no U.S. export licence. The exceptions are uranium and thorium ores (an NRC licence), hazardous slag and residues (EPA consent) and Russia and Belarus (a Commerce licence for every Chapter 26 code except uranium ores)."
  ],
